</Folder><Folder><name>Hyperspectral leads (EMIT)</name>
<Placemark><name>E1 jarosite/copiapite cluster</name><description><![CDATA[EMIT L2B: sulphate minerals in ~9 cells (60 m), identified on up to 4 of 5 dates (2023-04 → 2024-10). Elev 3,590–3,835 m, ~1 km from the red-bed margin. Sentinel-2 shows NO corroborating ferric/Al-OH anomaly. Jarosite also forms on pyritic black shale — not diagnostic of ore. Check on the ground.]]></description><styleUrl>#emit</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>72.27,36.209,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>E2 "azurite" scatter</name><description><![CDATA[EMIT library matches to azurite in 23 cells on ≥3 dates, scattered over ~1 km² near Buni. Unconfirmed: azurite has carbonate-like SWIR features, and the whole area is carbonate-rich.]]></description><styleUrl>#emit</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>72.289,36.262,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder><Folder><name>Published reference localities</name>
<Placemark><name>Charun Gol — type locality, Charun Quartzite</name><description><![CDATA[USGS OFR 75-556 (Stauffer 1975): white quartzite, ~250 ft, type section at lat 36°13′N, long 72°12′E (coordinates as published, minute precision).]]></description><styleUrl>#ref</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>72.2,36.2167,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kuragh fossil ridge (Hayden 1914)</name><description><![CDATA[Most prolific Devonian fossil locality, ridge <1 mile S of Kuragh (36°13′N 72°10′E as published).]]></description><styleUrl>#ref</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>72.1667,36.2167,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Damun Gol laterite (approx.)</name><description><![CDATA[USGS OFR 75-556: bright-red nodular lateritic layer 5–20 ft thick in Shogram Fm, ~1 mile due E of Reshun; 35% Al₂O₃, 16% Fe₂O₃, 10% FeO, 22% SiO₂. Position approximate (described, not surveyed).]]></description><styleUrl>#ref</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>72.116,36.154,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder></Document></kml>